---
import InfoLayout from "../layouts/InfoLayout.astro";
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, SITE_NAME } from "../lib/site";

const sections = [
  { slug: "report", text: "Reporting a problem" },
  { slug: "scope", text: "In scope" },
  { slug: "out-of-scope", text: "Out of scope" },
  { slug: "rules", text: "Testing safely" },
  { slug: "response", text: "What to expect" },
  { slug: "measures", text: "How the site is protected" },
  { slug: "thanks", text: "Thanks" },
] as const;
---

<InfoLayout path="/security/" sections={sections}>
  <h2 id="report">Reporting a problem</h2>
  <p>
    If you find a vulnerability in {SITE_NAME}, please email{" "}
    <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}?subject=Security`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>{" "}
    with “Security” in the subject, or use the{" "}
    <a href="/contact/">contact form</a> (choose “A security problem”). Include
    what you found, where, the steps to reproduce it, and what someone could do
    with it. Please don’t put working secrets, other people’s data or exploit
    code in the contact form. This policy is also listed in{" "}
    <a href="/.well-known/security.txt">security.txt</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="scope">In scope</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>www.trilleo.net and trilleo.net, including the API under /api/.</li>
    <li>files.trilleo.net, where published files are served.</li>
    <li>
      Sign-in, sessions, email addresses and their codes, unsubscribe links,
      comments, uploads and moderation, tool data, game saves and the Bazaar
      (including ways to cheat the server’s checks), and the Minecraft platform:
      projects, releases, 3D previews, embeds and its JSON API.
    </li>
    <li>The source code of this site on GitHub.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="out-of-scope">Out of scope</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      GitHub, Cloudflare, Huawei Cloud and Tencent Cloud themselves: report to
      them.
    </li>
    <li>Denial of service, load testing, spam, and social engineering.</li>
    <li>
      Reports from automated scanners without a demonstrated impact, missing
      headers with no exploit, and self-XSS.
    </li>
    <li>
      The Content-Security-Policy is report-only for now; that alone isn’t a
      finding.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="rules">Testing safely</h2>
  <p>
    Use your own accounts, and only test against data that is yours. Don’t
    access, change or delete anyone else’s data; stop and report as soon as you
    see some. Don’t degrade the site for others, and give me reasonable time to
    fix a problem before telling anyone else. If you follow this policy in good
    faith, I won’t take legal action against you, and will say so to anyone who
    asks.
  </p>

  <h2 id="response">What to expect</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A reply within 3 days, saying whether I could reproduce it.</li>
    <li>
      Serious problems fixed as fast as possible, usually within a week; you’ll
      hear when it’s done.
    </li>
    <li>
      Credit by name (or handle) here and in the fix, if you’d like it. There is
      no bug bounty: this is a personal, non-commercial site.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="measures">How the site is protected</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      HTTPS everywhere, behind Cloudflare; the server accepts connections only
      from Cloudflare.
    </li>
    <li>
      No passwords: one-time email codes (stored as hashes, limited guesses,
      short-lived), passkeys (WebAuthn, user verification required) or GitHub
      sign-in with PKCE. An address alone never links an outside account.
      Session tokens are stored only as hashes.
    </li>
    <li>
      Account owners are told about sign-ins from new browsers, and an address
      change can be undone from the old address, which also signs everyone out.
    </li>
    <li>
      Every change that alters data is a POST form with an origin check; pages
      with private data are never cached.
    </li>
    <li>
      Comment formatting allows no HTML, and only web links. Uploaded files are
      scanned for malware, and anything that could run in a browser is served as
      a download from a separate domain.
    </li>
    <li>
      Dependencies are kept up to date automatically, and code is scanned on
      every change.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="thanks">Thanks</h2>
  <p>No reports yet. Yours could be the first.</p>
</InfoLayout>
